          "description": "§6.1 step 1 and §7.4 rank 4. The table holds 83.279223744292 for source type 1059 in Michigan; the engine sees 83.3, because canonical writes the .POP file with %17.1f. Both numbers are asserted so that the quantization cannot be silently dropped -- it is a 0.02% change here and a 7.9% one on SCC 2265007015's second point. Reading these out of 62,699 rows rather than 13 also proves J5: Michigan alone is 83.28, Michigan plus Ohio would be 117.1, and the national total is larger again.\n\nBINARY32 QUANTITIES: every number this test pins is a floating-point quantity, so it asserts at one binary32 epsilon (2^-23) rather than the model's 1e-12 -- the resolution of the declared element type. The tests that pin a ten-digit key keep the exact default; the precision note in `metadata.description` says why a key must never be compared within a tolerance.",
