              "time": 0.0,
              "expected": 0.03225806451612903,
              "coords": {
                "run_scope_rows": 1
              }
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "the_ambient_temperature_is_thirteen_hours_of_one_zone",
          "description": "§6.0: zone 261610, month 8, hourID 6 through 18 inclusive -- THIRTEEN rows out of 930,816, summing to 956.5000152587901 and averaging 73.57692425067616 degF. The window is inclusive at both ends: twelve hours would give 73.98 and put tamb on the same side of the 75 degF branch, so the count is asserted as well as the mean. The float32 reference prints 73.576927; evaluated in float32 this document computes 73.5769271850586, and the thirteen-term sum accumulates to 4.8e-08 of the binary64 one -- a float32 reduction rounds once per accumulation step, not once at the end (esm-spec 11.3.1).\n\nBINARY32 QUANTITIES: every number this test pins is a floating-point quantity, so it asserts at one binary32 epsilon (2^-23) rather than the model's 1e-12 -- the resolution of the declared element type. The tests that pin a ten-digit key keep the exact default; the precision note in `metadata.description` says why a key must never be compared within a tolerance.",
          "time_span": {
            "start": 0.0,
            "end": 0.0
          },
          "tolerance": {
            "rel": 1.1920929e-07
          },
          "assertions": [
            {
              "variable": "Nonroad.run_daytimeHourCount",
              "time": 0.0,
              "expected": 13.0,
              "coords": {
                "run_scope_rows": 1
              }
            },
            {
              "variable": "Nonroad.run_daytimeTemperatureSum",
              "time": 0.0,
              "expected": 956.5000152587901,
              "coords": {
                "run_scope_rows": 1
              }
            },
            {
              "variable": "Nonroad.run_meanTemperature",
              "time": 0.0,
              "expected": 73.57692425067616,
              "coords": {
                "run_scope_rows": 1
              }
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "the_oxygen_content_survives_four_joins",
          "description": "§6.0: one gasoline supply row reaches this county-month -- formulation 9114, Gasohol E10, marketShare 1.0, 10 vol% ethanol at 0.3653 wt%/vol% -- giving oxy = 3.653. Four things would each give a plausible wrong number: dropping the regionCodeID = 2 join doubles it (the region appears twice), dropping the fuelYearID join reaches ~140 wt% (the capture carries 1990-2060), dropping the subtype join adds the diesel rows, and dropping the market-share weight gives 3.653 anyway HERE and not on a county with two formulations. The oxygen content is a binary32 quantity and carries a one-epsilon tolerance on its own assertion rather than on the test, because the same test pins `run_fuelRegionID = 270000000` -- a key, exact in binary32 as it happens, and a key must stay at the model's exact 1e-12 whether or not it rounds.",
          "time_span": {
            "start": 0.0,
            "end": 0.0
          },
          "assertions": [
            {
              "variable": "Nonroad.run_fuelYearID",
              "time": 0.0,
              "expected": 2020.0,
              "coords": {
                "run_scope_rows": 1
              }
            },
            {
              "variable": "Nonroad.run_fuelRegionID",
              "time": 0.0,
              "expected": 270000000.0,
              "coords": {
                "run_scope_rows": 1
              }
            },
            {
              "variable": "Nonroad.run_oxygenWeightPercent",
              "time": 0.0,
              "expected": 3.653,
              "coords": {
                "run_scope_rows": 1
              },
              "tolerance": {
                "rel": 1.1920929e-07
              }
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "the_two_stroke_adjustment_is_the_one_selected",
          "description": "§6.0's adjustment table for a 2260 SCC: THC 0.978082, CO 0.762555, NOx 1.679458, PM 1.0. Every two-stroke temperature coefficient is zero, so the whole gasoline adjustment here is the oxygenate term -- and that is exactly why the engine fuel kind has to be right: the four-stroke row set at the same tamb and oxy gives 0.8384738, 0.7717785 and 1.4382362, all plausible.\n\nBINARY32 QUANTITIES: every number this test pins is a floating-point quantity, so it asserts at one binary32 epsilon (2^-23) rather than the model's 1e-12 -- the resolution of the declared element type. The tests that pin a ten-digit key keep the exact default; the precision note in `metadata.description` says why a key must never be compared within a tolerance.\n\nThe point axis is now explicit in the coordinates, and it has to be: five of the six equipment points are FOUR-stroke and take the other four rows of the coefficient table. The two-stroke numbers below are point 1's.",
