        {
          "id": "the_growth_series_is_read_year_by_year_and_interpolated",
          "description": "docs/nonroad-logging-county.md §6.0 and §2.2(b). agedist folds THIRTY ANNUAL FACTORS, not one cumulative ratio, and until this commit the fixture read only the two endpoint rows. Now it reads getgrw's whole series for the run's pattern, and each of the three cases in that series is asserted:\n\nAN EXACT TABLE ROW. 1990 and 2020 are tabulated, so the bracketing years coincide and the indicator is the stored value, 321 and 1508.\n\nAN INTERPOLATED YEAR. Pattern 2176 jumps from 1990 straight to 1999, so 1991 is bracketed by 1990 (321) and 1999 (1481) and getgrw returns 449.8889 -- the number §6.0 gives, and the number scrptime's sales curve is built from. `fold_lowYearID` and `fold_highYearID` are asserted at that row so the bracket itself is pinned, not just its result: an interpolation between the wrong two rows would land somewhere plausible.\n\nA YEAR-OVER-YEAR DIP. The indicator falls from 1481 in 1999 to 1000 in 2000, so grwfac(1999, 2000) is NEGATIVE, -0.32478055. That sign is the mechanism §6.3 names as the single most fragile behaviour in the chain: it is what drives agedist's unclamped residual below zero and makes the model-year sets gappy. A cumulative-ratio port would never see it, and a port that took the absolute value or clamped the factor would pass every other assertion in this file.\n\nBINARY32 QUANTITIES for the indicators and factors; the years are keys and stay exact.",
