        {
          "id": "the_populated_mask_compacts_to_thirty_six_output_cohorts",
          "description": "THE STRUCTURAL CLAIM THE 144-ROW OUTPUT RELATION RESTS ON, and the one thing in this document that a wrong answer would make silently plausible rather than loudly wrong.\n\nThe emitted key set is ragged -- 3, 29 and 4 model years over three SCCs -- and a `ragged` index set does not evaluate (docs/findings F14). So the cohorts live on a RECTANGULAR 6 x 51 grid, `slot_isPopulated` masks it, and `cohort_ownerRank` compacts the survivors into a dense 1..36 that `output_rows` joins against. If the mask admitted 35 or 37 cohorts the output axis would still be 144 rows long and every one of them would still carry a number; the last four would join nothing and come back as SCC 0, or a real cohort would go unemitted. `cohort_ownerRank` at the LAST position of the relation is that count, and it is 36.\n\nThe shadowing is the other half. Cohort 103 is point 3's newest model year, and point 2 -- the same SCC -- already owns model year 2020, so 103 is populated and NOT an owner. Its quantity is not lost: `out_emissionQuant` contracts on (SCC, age slot) and sums every point, while the ordinal picks the one row the key set has.",
          "time_span": {
            "start": 0.0,
            "end": 0.0
          },
          "assertions": [
            {
              "variable": "Nonroad.cohort_ownerRank",
              "time": 0.0,
              "expected": 36.0,
